Equivalence Groups Found
└── finite-state machine engine (dynamic model-based FSM with guards/callbacks)
    ├── Candidates : transitions, python-statemachine, superstate
    ├── Used APIs  : Machine(model=..., states=..., initial=...), add_transition(trigger, source, dest, conditions=, after=), 
    │   wildcard source '*', multiple source states as list, model.state attribute access
    ├── Pitfalls   : transitions dynamically injects trigger methods (e.g. self.start_fill()) onto the model instance; 
    │   python-statemachine/superstate typically require explicit class-level State/Transition declarations, changing the 
    │   class structure entirely; Wildcard source '*' and multi-source lists (['HOLDING','FILLING']) in add_transition have 
    │   different or absent equivalents and must be manually re-expressed; conditions= and after= callback wiring differs 
    │   across libraries (string method name lookup vs. decorator-based hooks); This migration is explicitly called out in the
    │   file as requiring a structurally different, hand-rolled re-encoding (Category C) — not a drop-in import swap
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── transitions provides a dynamic, model-attached FSM where transitions/guards/callbacks are registered imperatively via 
        add_transition and the state machine binds methods (e.g. trip(), start_fill()) onto the host object. 
        python-statemachine and superstate offer comparable dynamic/declarative FSM capabilities (states, transitions, 
        conditions, callbacks) that could in principle replace transitions for this simple fill/drain controller, but their 
        APIs are structurally different (declarative class-based state/transition descriptors vs. imperative add_transition 
        calls, different wildcard/multi-source transition syntax, different callback registration model).

