    │   string-based guard resolution would need to be re-wired; The wildcard transition ('*' -> 'FAULT') syntax is 
    │   transitions-specific and must be manually re-implemented in an alternative library; conditions/after are passed as 
    │   method-name strings resolved against the model in transitions; other libraries may require actual callables or 
    │   decorators instead; python-statemachine and automat require converting this composition-based design into class 
    │   inheritance or decorator-annotated classes, which the docstring explicitly flags as requiring a hand-rolled rewrite 
    │   (Category C)
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── transitions binds a Machine instance to an arbitrary model object and dynamically injects trigger methods (start_fill,
        trip, etc.) based on add_transition() calls with conditions/after callbacks resolved by name against the model. fysom 
        offers a similar dynamic, dict/kwargs-driven transition table with callback binding, making it the closest functional 
        analog. python-statemachine and automat instead require declarative class-based state/transition definitions (class 
        attributes or decorators), which is a fundamentally different architecture from the dynamic model-binding pattern used
        here, so they are rejected as candidates despite being generic FSM libraries.

